    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "#### Now that we’ve learned about how LLMs work, let’s take a look at some examples of LLMs and Generative AI that are popular today.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<details style=\"background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;\">\r\n    <summary style=\"font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;\">📝 Key Points from the Video</summary>\r\n<div style=\"margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; padding-bottom:20px;\">\r\n    <p>\r\n\r\n#### Current Popular LLMs:\r\n* #### ChatGPT: Developed by OpenAI, this large language model has evolved through various iterations, with GPT-4 being the latest and most advanced. ChatGPT, including version 3.5, operates based on data up to 2021 and does not access the internet.\r\n* #### Google Bard (now Gemini): Powered by a streamlined version of Google's Lambda model, Bard functions similarly to ChatGPT, focusing on generating responses based on a fixed dataset without real-time internet access.\r\n* #### Microsoft's Bing Chatbot (now Copilot): This AI utilizes technology similar to ChatGPT but includes internet connectivity, allowing it to pull current information and update its responses accordingly.\r\n* #### Beyond text, generative AI also extends to creating unique visual content. Tools like DALL-E or MidJourney can generate novel images based on user prompts, expanding creative possibilities.\r\n\r\n#### Prompt Engineering is a technique that involves refining and adjusting the inputs given to AI models to achieve more accurate or desired outputs.\r\n\r\n</p></div>\r\n</details>\r\n"
  },
